<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bono people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Abron, Brong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bono, also called Abron, are an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> people of west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Côte d'Ivoire</a>. Their state of Bono-Manso is regarded as one of the earliest Akan kingdoms, and groups of Bono migrants moving west from the late 17th century founded the Kingdom of Gyaman around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bondoukou">Bondoukou</a>. Like other Akan peoples they trace royal descent through the mother's line.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bono-people.jpg" alt="Bono people" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kamara kolo rachelle · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danseuse_Abron.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Bono is derived from Abono woo, pioneer or firstborn of the land. Techiman-Bono tradition holds that the first ancestors emerged from a sacred cave called Amowi, near present-day Techiman, under Asaman, the first ruler, and founded a settlement called Yefiri. The colonial-era scholar Eva Meyerowitz argued that the Bono came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> by way of a place called Diala; Bono oral historians reject this, and the historian Kwasi Konadu finds no evidence of an intrusive northern people, pointing to iron-smelting and settlement at Amowi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bono-manso">Bono Manso</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/begho">Begho</a> dating back to at least 300 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bono-manso">Bono Manso</a>, the capital, was organised as a confederation of matrilineal clans with roles as priests, warriors or administrators. From the 12th century the state prospered on the gold of the Twi and Prabom valleys in the Tain River basin, and gold dust served as currency in trade with Djenne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. The market town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/begho">Begho</a>, which at its peak had an estimated 15,000 people, had quarters for Bono, for Mande-speaking Muslim traders and for metalworkers. Bono Manso also became involved in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>.</p><p>In 1722/23 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opoku-ware-i">Opoku Ware I</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bono-manso">Bono Manso</a>, and the survivors moved their capital to Techiman, which was destroyed again around 1877 after its ruler Kwabena Fofie refused to join an Asante war. Bono chiefs left the Asante Confederacy in 1952, and in 1959 the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> created the Brong-Ahafo Region, separate from the Ashanti Region, with its own House of Chiefs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Bono speak Bono Twi and are one of the largest matrilineal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> groups. The queenmother nominates the king and keeps the royal line within the maternal clan, and the two act together as spiritual and administrative heads. Society is organised in seven or eight matrilineal clans, each with its own totems, taboos and origin stories. Their royal regalia include ceremonial stools, umbrellas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> trumpets and gold swords, and Kete, Adowa and fontomfrom music accompany festivals, funerals and royal ceremonies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bono people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gyaman was founded in the late 17th century by <b>Abron</b> (Bono) migrants under their leader Tan Date, who moved west from the Bono area to escape the rising power of the Ashanti Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gyaman">Kingdom of Gyaman</a></li></ul></section>
